<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-18" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Fixed Axis Rotation</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        <alert>Note:</alert> we will use this worksheet for a few lectures,
        so keep it with you.
    </p>
   </introduction>

  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <p>
        Let's see how the rotation of the pulley affects the motion of the Atwood
        machine.
        The problem is to find the acceleration <m>a</m> of the masses for the
        arrangement shown in the sketch.
        We will assume that the rope does not slip on the pulley, so as the masses
        accelerate, the pulley will also have some angular acceleration
        <m>\alpha</m>.
        The rope will still be taken to be massless.
    </p>

    <image width="50%" source="images\activities\lec18\atwood_massive.png"/>
    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Draw free body diagrams for the masses and the pulley.
                    In your force diagram for the pulley, do not treat it as a
                    point: show the forces where they are applied on it.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Discussion:</alert> Looking at your FBD for the pulley,
                    explain why the tension in the string cannot be constant on the
                    two ends touching the pulley.
                    How does this not contradict our previous discussions on
                    massless strings?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How does the rolling without slipping constraint relate <m>a</m>
                    and <m>\alpha</m>?
                    For <m>\tau_{z}</m> to be positive out of the page,
                    <m>\alpha</m> is positive counterclockwise.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Write Newton's second law for the masses, and
                    <m>\tau_{z} = \dfrac{dL_z}{dt}</m> for the pulley, with the
                    origin taken to be the center of the pulley.
                    <alert>DO NOT SOLVE THE EQUATIONS.</alert>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Check that with the three equations above and the rolling
                    without slipping constraint, you have enough equations to solve
                    for all of the unknown quantities.
                    Solve these out of class (KK Example 7.10 if you need help).
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
   <p> 
        A hollow cylinder, a solid cylinder, a solid sphere, and a 
        hollow sphere all roll without slipping down an incline. 
        Which of the shapes wins the race?
    </p>  
    </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        A body is given a sharp kick by a force F acting along the line
        <m>\gamma</m> (see picture).
        When is the angular momentum *relative to point A* conserved?
    </p>

    <image width="50%" source="images\activities\lec18\sharp_kick.png"/>
    <ol cols="2">
        <li>
            <p>
               When F is conservative 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                When F does no work
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Whenever the line passes through the center of mass
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Always
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Never
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>

  </exercise>

  <exercise>
        <p>
        A heavy frying pan is lying on a flat, frictionless table.
        View from above.
        Which quantities remain constant?
        Select all.
    </p>

    <image width="50%" source="images\activities\lec18\frying_pan.png"/>
    <p>
        <ol cols="2">
            <li>
                <p>
                    Kinetic Energy
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Momentum Along X
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Momentum Along Y
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Angular Momentum About H
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Angular Momentum About CM
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Total Momentum, defined as P + L
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
       <p>
           As the wheel transitions from the sloped to the horizontal portion of its
           trajectory, which quantities remain constant?
           Select all.
       </p>

       <image source="images\activities\lec18\toothed_wheel.png"/>
       <p>
           <ol cols="2">
               <li>
                   <p>
                       Total Mechanical Energy
                   </p>
               </li>

               <li>
                   <p>
                       Rotational Kinetic Energy
                   </p>
               </li>

               <li>
                   <p>
                       Horizontal Momentum
                   </p>
               </li>

               <li>
                   <p>
                       Angular Momentum About A
                   </p>
               </li>

               <li>
                   <p>
                       Angular Momentum About B
                   </p>
               </li>

               <li>
                   <p>
                       Angular Momentum About C
                   </p>
               </li>
           </ol>
       </p>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>